WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.899
Add one to a long row of nines, and every column flips.

00:00:03.899 --> 00:00:05.881
Why do language models lose that carry?

00:00:05.881 --> 00:00:08.430
And how does a tiny model never lose it?

00:00:10.832 --> 00:00:11.961
Hi, I'm Zephyrine!

00:00:11.961 --> 00:00:16.854
This is Sketchpad: exact long arithmetic from a tiny model, with a certificate.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:19.658
Chapter one.

00:00:19.658 --> 00:00:21.180
Why the carry gets lost.

00:00:25.275 --> 00:00:28.981
A model trained on short sums usually struggles on long ones.

00:00:28.981 --> 00:00:31.003
The literature points at three suspects.

00:00:31.423 --> 00:00:31.803
Position.

00:00:31.803 --> 00:00:36.366
A model trained on twenty-digit sums has never seen column three hundred.

00:00:36.786 --> 00:00:37.336
The carry.

00:00:37.336 --> 00:00:40.637
One carry can depend on every digit to its right, and a

00:00:40.637 --> 00:00:42.012
forward pass has fixed depth.

00:00:42.432 --> 00:00:44.390
Chain of thought writes steps down.

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:48.305
But step nine hundred reads nine hundred steps of history nobody tested.

00:00:49.967 --> 00:00:50.526
Chapter two.

00:00:50.526 --> 00:00:51.925
A sheet and a window.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.673
The system has three parts.

00:00:58.673 --> 00:01:01.671
The sheet holds everything: operands, carries, the answer.

00:01:01.671 --> 00:01:04.668
The runtime crops one column, three cells tall.

00:01:05.088 --> 00:01:08.807
Qwen three point five, zero point eight billion, with a small adapter,

00:01:08.807 --> 00:01:10.667
reads that strip as an image.

00:01:10.667 --> 00:01:12.526
Nothing else is in its prompt.

00:01:12.946 --> 00:01:15.787
It answers a digit, a carry, and the next operation.

00:01:16.207 --> 00:01:19.053
Plain code paints the answer and moves the window.

00:01:19.053 --> 00:01:22.214
The model is the transition function of a Turing machine.

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:25.349
The project's own animation.

00:01:25.349 --> 00:01:27.947
The window reads three cells of one column.

00:01:28.367 --> 00:01:31.423
The model answers a digit, a carry and the next step.

00:01:31.423 --> 00:01:33.923
The runtime stamps it, and the window moves on.

00:01:35.575 --> 00:01:39.141
Here's the hard case, the one that breaks a fixed-depth forward pass:

00:01:39.141 --> 00:01:40.329
nine nines, plus one.

00:01:40.749 --> 00:01:44.987
The window shows the first column: no carry, a nine over a one.

00:01:44.987 --> 00:01:46.617
The model answers one token.

00:01:46.617 --> 00:01:48.573
a means digit zero, carry one.

00:01:48.573 --> 00:01:52.812
The runtime writes the zero, puts the carry one column left, and moves.

00:01:53.232 --> 00:01:55.798
Now the view is a carry over a nine.

00:01:55.798 --> 00:01:58.934
Same kind of step, again and again, all the way along.

00:01:58.934 --> 00:02:01.500
The carry chain is never computed inside one pass.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.211
It is written on the sheet.

00:02:03.631 --> 00:02:05.525
The last column is just the carry.

00:02:05.525 --> 00:02:08.501
Then all three cells are blank, and the model says halt.

00:02:09.967 --> 00:02:10.742
Chapter three.

00:02:10.742 --> 00:02:11.905
Checking every view.

00:02:16.142 --> 00:02:17.192
Here's the idea.

00:02:17.192 --> 00:02:19.640
Every step reads only a three-cell view.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:23.489
So checking every possible view checks every run, of every length.

00:02:25.217 --> 00:02:28.727
In addition, the carry is blank or one, and each digit cell

00:02:28.727 --> 00:02:30.190
is blank or a digit.

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:32.530
Two hundred and forty-two views, at any length.

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:37.234
Multiplication's five tables add one thousand five hundred and fifty-three.

00:02:37.654 --> 00:02:42.373
Subtraction, estimation for division, and a decrement add six hundred and fifty-one.

00:02:42.793 --> 00:02:45.778
Two thousand four hundred and forty-six views, in all.

00:02:47.067 --> 00:02:48.656
Every step has the same shape.

00:02:49.076 --> 00:02:51.369
The runtime shows a view, from a finite list.

00:02:52.706 --> 00:02:54.458
The answer depends on that view alone.

00:02:56.141 --> 00:03:00.411
So matching the table on every view means matching every run, step by step.

00:03:02.217 --> 00:03:03.312
Checked means a margin.

00:03:03.312 --> 00:03:06.325
Every view is scored on the device, and each must put

00:03:06.325 --> 00:03:07.421
the right token ahead.

00:03:07.841 --> 00:03:10.627
All two thousand four hundred and forty-six pass.

00:03:10.627 --> 00:03:14.805
The weakest, five plus six, leads by three point zero two logits.

00:03:16.092 --> 00:03:17.739
Why not just test sums?

00:03:17.739 --> 00:03:19.715
The article broke that one view.

00:03:19.715 --> 00:03:23.338
Ten-digit sums still came out right ninety-four percent of the time.

00:03:23.338 --> 00:03:25.644
Enumerating the views catches it with certainty.

00:03:27.217 --> 00:03:27.877
Chapter four.

00:03:27.877 --> 00:03:29.198
What the proof covers.

00:03:32.983 --> 00:03:34.926
Chain of thought grows its context.

00:03:34.926 --> 00:03:36.546
No finite test certifies it.

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:39.257
The pinned sheet has a fixed list of inputs.

00:03:39.257 --> 00:03:40.013
Check them all.

00:03:42.217 --> 00:03:45.719
The paper proves it in Lean: the model's run equals the reference

00:03:45.719 --> 00:03:49.513
run, for every length, if and only if it agrees on every view.

00:03:49.933 --> 00:03:53.000
And when a step can read its own history, a decoder can

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:56.068
pass every test up to some length, then fail just past it.

00:03:57.525 --> 00:03:58.742
So what did Lean prove?

00:03:58.742 --> 00:04:01.664
A chain of three links, and only the middle one is formal.

00:04:02.084 --> 00:04:06.116
The model is right on every view: a measurement, on one GPU path.

00:04:06.536 --> 00:04:10.065
Right on every view implies right at every length: the Lean theorem.

00:04:10.485 --> 00:04:15.375
And the runtime only ever shows table views: assumed, and asserted at every step.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.984
Bound what each step can see, and exactness at every length becomes

00:04:19.984 --> 00:04:21.675
a finite list you can check.

00:04:24.817 --> 00:04:26.687
Three cells in, one step out.

00:04:26.687 --> 00:04:29.179
Check every view, and induction does the rest.

00:04:29.179 --> 00:04:30.114
Thanks for watching!

00:04:30.534 --> 00:04:32.766
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:32.766 --> 00:04:33.404
I'm Zephyrine.

00:04:33.404 --> 00:04:33.723
Bye!
